Define E_X = set of X-regular extensions of the initial data (extensions with regularity X). C2 ⊆ C0 (as function spaces), so E_C2 ⊆ E_C0. If E_C2 is empty (C2-inextendible), does that imply E_C0 empty (C0-inextendible)? No! A smaller set being empty does not imply the bigger set is empty. Conversely, E_C0 empty implies E_C2 empty (if there's no continuous extension at all, there's no C2 extension). So C0-inextendibility ⇒ C2-inextendibility. I.e., the implication runs C0 => C2, and C0-inextendibility is the *stronger* statement. Yes — that matches the FROZEN repairs: "C0-inextendibility is stronger", "the implication runs C0 => C2 only". Good.

